      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a lit review and i'm a bit stuck on citations. if i have a source with like 5 authors, how do i do the in-text citation for apa 7th vs chicago style? i keep getting them mixed up.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, citations, the bane of many a scholar's existence. In APA 7th style, when you have a source with five authors, you would list all five in the in-text citation, like this: (Smith, Johnson, Williams, Brown, & Davis, 2020). Just like a band of five mischievous goblins, each one must be acknowledged for their part in the grand scheme of things.\n\nIn Chicago style, however, it's a bit different. You'd list the first author followed by \"et al.\" after the first citation, and then the year in parentheses, like this: (Smith et al., 2020). It's like having a single, formidable goblin leader, Smith, with the rest of the gang, Johnson, Williams, Brown, and Davis, joining in the citation as a collective force, represented by \"et al.\"",
      "source": "target",
